- How would you scale this to n variables and multiple formulas?

## Why This Is Asked
To gauge practical reasoning about logic constraints, Horn-SAT familiarity, and ability to reason about linear-time algorithms and incremental updates in data pipelines.

## Key Concepts
- Horn clauses
- Forward chaining
- Partial vs full assignments
- Complexity O(n+m)
- Cycle handling and inconsistency detection

## Code Example
```javascript
function solveHorn(rules, facts){
  const trueSet = new Set(facts);
  let queue = rules.filter(r => r.antecedent.every(v => trueSet.has(v)));
  while (queue.length){
    const r = queue.shift();
    if (!trueSet.has(r.consequent)){
      trueSet.add(r.consequent);
      queue = rules.filter(x => x.antecedent.every(v => trueSet.has(v)) && !trueSet.has(x.consequent));
    }
  }
  return trueSet;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to non-Horn clauses?
- How would you handle incremental updates when facts change?

## Why This Is Asked
This probes designing a 3-valued constraint propagator with a nontrivial rule and a forced Unknown branching, mirroring how production systems reason about partial information.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued logic (H>U> N) and definition of implication as ¬A ∨ B
- Constraint propagation and fixpoint computation
- Backtracking on U choices to avoid definite F outcomes

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal 3-valued logic helpers
const order = {H:2, U:1, N:0};
function not(v){ return v==='H'?'N': v==='N'?'H':'U'; }
function and(a,b){ return order[a] <= order[b] ? a : b; }
function or(a,b){ return order[a] >= order[b] ? a : b; }
function implies(a,b){ return or(not(a), b); }
